><title>Science as Verifiable Search</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-01-05-science-as-verifiable-search/</link><pubDate>Sun, 05 Jan 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-01-05-science-as-verifiable-search/</guid><description>Science is search through hypothesis space. Intelligence prunes; testing provides signal. Synthetic worlds could accelerate the loop.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-01-05-science-as-verifiable-search/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Research</media:title></media:content><category>AI</category><category>machine-learning</category><category>science</category><category>reinforcement-learning</category><category>epistemology</category></item><item><title>Fuzzy Soft Circuits: Learning Fuzzy Rules from Data</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-10-01-fuzzy-soft-circuits/</link><pubDate>Tue, 01 Oct 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-10-01-fuzzy-soft-circuits/</guid><description>What if fuzzy logic systems could discover their own rules? An interactive demo of differentiable fuzzy circuits that learn membership functions, rule structure, and rule existence, all via gradient descent.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-10-01-fuzzy-soft-circuits/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Research</media:title></media:content><category>machine-learning</category><category>fuzzy-logic</category><category>differentiable-programming</category><category>interactive</category></item><item><title>A Boolean Algebra Over Trapdoors</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/trapdoor-boolean-model/</link><pubDate>Sat, 17 Jun 2023 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/trapdoor-boolean-model/</guid><description>A Boolean algebra framework over trapdoors for cryptographic operations. Introduces a homomorphism from powerset Boolean algebra to n-bit strings via cryptographic hash functions, enabling secure computations with one-way properties.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/trapdoor-boolean-model/featured.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Research</media:title></media:content><category>cryptography</category><category>boolean-algebra</category><category>probabilistic-data-structures</category><category>bernoulli-model</category></item><item><title>The Bernoulli Model: A Probabilistic Framework for Data Structures and Types</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/bernoulli-boolean-model/</link><pubDate>Sat, 17 Jun 2023 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/bernoulli-boolean-model/</guid><description>The Bernoulli Model is a framework for reasoning about probabilistic data structures by treating noisy outputs as Bernoulli-distributed approximations of latent values, from Booleans to set-indicator functions.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/bernoulli-boolean-model/featured.png" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Research</media:title></media:content><category>probabilistic-data-structures</category><category>bloom-filter</category><category>probability</category><category>type-theory</category><category>bernoulli-model</category></item></channel></rss>